{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1559054-help-me-with-this-rote","id":1559054,"title":"Help me with this Rote.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2026-03-28T06:25:53-07:00","id":1559054,"title":"Help me with this Rote.","content":"This rote is a simple idea. It's meant to use Forces and Life to gently agitate the fluid of the inner ear.<br /> <br /> A very light use simply disrupts balance. This would break confidence and initiative. There would be a risk that if the target of the rote acted to quickly they might lose balance all together. The effects wouldn't last long.<br /> <br /> A slightly heavier touch would force the target to stand still or sit. The effects would last longer but a couple of minutes tops.<br /> <br /> A stronger use of this rote would simply knock the target down and leave them unable to pursue or take any involved actions for about five minutes.<br /> <br /> This is meant to be a self defense rote. It's not supposed to do damage (although the consequences of the induced vertigo could indirectly do damage).<br /> <br /> This rote is supposed to be subtle and avoid <b>Paradox</b> by leaving no marks or direct damage and by attacking in an ambiguous manner that could just be the target getting ill.<br /> <br /> How do I need to describe/document this rote idea?<br /> <br /> An example of indirect damage might be a thug running up a staircase to attack the caster being hit with this rote and, because they are too dizzy to stand, they fall down the stairs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":857,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","user":"Astromancer","date":". 03-28-2026, 09:29 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-28T08:28:17-07:00","id":1559060,"title":"","content":"Sounds like a Life 3 or Life 3/Forces 2 spell. Life 3 can do this on its own. Forces can\u2019t target only part of a pattern (inner ear) specifically without Life, but Forces gets an extra success on attack rolls. I\u2019d have to check the wording but it might apply here.<br /> <br /> It sounds like the mechanics would be a typical debuff format, which I think is +1 difficulty for the target to make certain rolls. There should be examples in M20 that will demonstrate that.<br /> <br /> The very light level would apply to initiative rolls and any action that requires balance.<br /> <br /> The slightly heavier level would expand application to any roll that requires standing or moving and the mage would also have to pay successes for duration.<br /> <br /> The heaviest use would apply to any physical rolls and the mage would have to pay successes for scene long duration. That step up might be a step up on the magical feats chart and require one extra success.<br /> <br /> As an effect it should naturally be coincidental. Successive uses could eventually tip over into vulgar, but given that there are easy explanations for a lot of people getting vertigo at once (e.g. a gas leak) it may not.<br /> <br /> is that what you were looking for?<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-28T09:30:47-07:00","id":1559062,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1559054-help-me-with-this-rote?p=1559060#post1559060\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Sounds like a Life 3 or Life 3/Forces 2 spell. Life 3 can do this on its own. Forces can\u2019t target only part of a pattern (inner ear) specifically without Life, but Forces gets an extra success on attack rolls. I\u2019d have to check the wording but it might apply here.<br /> <br /> It sounds like the mechanics would be a typical debuff format, which I think is +1 difficulty for the target to make certain rolls. There should be examples in M20 that will demonstrate that.<br /> <br /> The very light level would apply to initiative rolls and any action that requires balance.<br /> <br /> The slightly heavier level would expand application to any roll that requires standing or moving and the mage would also have to pay successes for duration.<br /> <br /> The heaviest use would apply to any physical rolls and the mage would have to pay successes for scene long duration. That step up might be a step up on the magical feats chart and require one extra success.<br /> <br /> As an effect it should naturally be coincidental. Successive uses could eventually tip over into vulgar, but given that there are easy explanations for a lot of people getting vertigo at once (e.g. a gas leak) it may not.<br /> <br /> is that what you were looking for?<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Yes. Pretty much. I wanted to be sure I didn't need a Prime element. <br /> <br /> It's meant to be coincidential and not too violent. Unless there isn't another option. <br /> <br /> Do you think tossing out an odd smelling &quot;stink bomb&quot; variation would make targeting a crowd coincidental. The bomb would be smoke and a chemical smell and completely mundane. The point would be making people think they'd been gassed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-28T11:26:39-07:00","id":1559067,"title":"","content":"Oh, I realize I left two words out. I think it is +1 difficulty <i>per success</i> for the target on appropriate rolls.<br /> <br /> No Prime that I can see. Life is manipulating what is already there, and Forces 2 is redirecting the forces that are already present as well. You <i>could</i> make a version that makes new Forces, and that would require Prime, but there\u2019s no benefit to doing so.<br /> <br /> A stink bomb is an excellent cover for this effect. If the scene is indoors, asking \u201cdo you smell gas?\u201d would also work well.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}